window.__BUNDLE__={"generatedAt":"2026-11-19T02:51:04.072376+00:00","recentDays":7,"archiveDays":1825,"recentCutoff":"2026-11-12","archiveCutoff":"2021-11-20","addedToday":82,"addedThisRun":82,"fetchStats":{"hf":210,"arxiv":1344,"total":1554},"freshness":{"latestPaperDate":"2026-11-19","latestPublishedDate":"2026-11-19","latestBatchCount":1,"newInLast24h":438,"newInLast48h":1097},"historyTotal":33919,"topicCounts":{"Multi-Agent":3434,"Evaluation":18679,"Planning":2434,"Memory":654,"Code Agent":1387,"Interpretability":1135,"Agentic RAG":1659,"Agentic Search":2244,"RL for LLM":1712,"Reasoning":8860,"Hallucination":1407,"Post-Training":3621,"Alignment":4374,"World Model":679,"Tool Use":1539,"Agent Safety":2887,"Computer Use":427,"Reflection":1231,"VLM Training":2766,"LLM Training":4358,"Fine-tuning":5248,"Multimodal Agent":198,"Agent Framework":1034,"Error Attribution":208,"Data Engine":681},"count":36,"recentTotal":1739,"archiveCount":0,"archiveTotal":32180,"sources":{"hf":35,"arxiv":1704,"semanticScholar":0},"archiveSources":{"hf":0,"arxiv":0,"semanticScholar":0},"warnings":[],"notes":["archive is split by year under data/archive/ for fast lazy loading; data/history.json keeps the complete database"],"papers":[{"topics":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23388","title":"QDA: Closed-Loop LLM Quantization Design with Agents","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23388","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23388.pdf","tags":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"authors":["Xingyang Li","Tianlang Zhao","Song Han","Ligeng Zhu"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.23388","source":"hf","date":"2026-11-19","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.23388","abstract":"Post-training quantization is the standard way to serve large language models at low cost. However, the best quantization recipe differs across models and precisions. As a result, experts must redesign it for every new model, which is time-consuming and often sub-optimal. Fortunately, an LLM agent can take over this design by refining the recipe over rounds of building and evaluating candidates, so that more rounds lead to better recipes. However, evaluating one quantized reasoning model takes tens of GPU-hours, and combining methods from different codebases means porting each to the new architecture by hand. We present QDA, a system in which an agent designs the recipe for each model and precision. To make evaluation fast, QDA scores each candidate in minutes by its output KL divergence from the original model on reasoning traces that the agent never sees, and checks promising ones with"},{"topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22115","title":"TeleChat to Xing4: Advancing Frontier Agentic Models on Domestic Chips and Frameworks","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22115","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22115.pdf","tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"authors":["Xingchen AGI Lab",":","Baotian Hu","Bingkai Yang","Boyang Wang","Changzai Pan","Chao Lyu","Chao Wang","Chen Li","Chenshuo Pan","Chenyu Lin","Chenyuan Zhang","Chongming Liu","Dawei Fan","Di Wu","Dong Guo","Dong Li","Dongfang Li","Enlin Liu","Fanye Xiao","Fengkai Li","Fubei Yao","Gang Lin","Han Gao","Hanming Wu","Haowei He","Huapeng Liu","Hua Wang","Huinan Xu","Jiahong Xiong","Jiaxin Peng","Jianxiang Ma","Jianxing Zhuge","Jie Zhang","Ji Xiong","Jinglin Chen","Jingqi Li","Jingxuan Li","Jiwang Ye","Junchen Li","Junbiao Li","Junyang Wu","Kaidong Yu","Kaipeng Jia","Kehai Chen","Kuai Li","Laobin Liu","Lei Li","Libo Qin","Lingling Shi","Luwen Pu","Menghao Xu","Mengjiao Bao","Mengqi Chen","Min Zhang","Mingrui Wang","Naying Wang","Peicong Han","Peiming Guo","Pengfei Li","Qiang Feng","Qi Wang","Qingjun Zhu","Qingli Li","Qitai Ding","Qiyi Xie","Quanchao Zhao","Rongbao Han","Rui Xue","Ruiyu Fang","Ruiting Song","Shijie Li","Shiquan Wang","Shuangyong Song","Sishi Xiong","Teng Su","Wankang Bao","Weijie Zhang","Wei Mao","Weiwei Fu","Weiyang Huang","Wenmin Deng","Wenqing Hu","Wujie Zhong","Xi Yu","Xiaohui Hu","Xiaomin Fang","Xiaomeng Huang","Xiaoqiong Shi","Xiaotong Shang","Xiaoyu Li","Xinglei Xu","Xin Jiang","Xin Wang","Xin Zhang","Xinzhang Liu","Xue Liu","Xuefeng Bai","Xuewei He","Yahan Li","Yanhan Zhang","Yanlei Xue","Yanzhang Wu","Yang Yu","Yi Liu","Yin Zhang","Yingyan Li","Yitong Yao","Yixuan Li","Yongqiang Xia","Yongxiang Li","Yinzhou Zhu","Yu Zhao","Yuhao Jiang","Yunpeng Zhang","Yuting Lin","Yuxuan Wu","Yuyao Huang","Yuyi Wang","Zebo Peng","Zhenwu Pei","Zhenxiong Li","Zhao Wang","Zhaoxi Zhang","Zhihao Yang","Zhiqiang Dong","Zhiwei Yan","Zhilong Lu","Zhonghao Guo","Zhongheng Wu","Zhongjiang He","Zhongqiu Li","Zhu Yuan","Zhuoru Jiang","Zhuoyi Wei","Zijia Guo","Zilu Huang","Zhipeng Guo","Zirui Li","Zihan Wang"],"upvotes":150,"id":"arxiv:2611.22115","source":"hf","date":"2026-11-18","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.22115","abstract":"We present Xing 4, a series of large language models that extends the TeleChat lineage through coordinated advances in model architecture and training infrastructure on Ascend. This report describes the design principles and systems engineering behind the series, spanning architecture selection, large-scale pretraining, and reasoning and agentic post-training. We study how sparse activation, attention design, model depth and width, and speculative decoding balance model capacity with computation, memory access, and execution overhead. Our pretraining infrastructure builds on MindSpore HyperParallel, combining topology-aware hybrid sharding, efficient distributed optimizer execution, and coordinated management of long-sequence workloads and activation memory. CPU affinity and core isolation further reduce host scheduling interference. Together, these mechanisms support the pretraining of "},{"topics":["Post-Training","VLM Training","Multi-Agent","Planning","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22275","title":"Heave-Ho: Decentralized Long-Horizon Multi-Humanoid Collaboration","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22275","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22275.pdf","tags":["Post-Training","VLM Training","Multi-Agent","Planning","Evaluation"],"authors":["Ting-Hsuan Chen","Siheng Zhao","Han Jiang","Qi Pan","Louison Lu","Jiaheng Luo","Henghui Bao","Yiyu Chen","Quan Nguyen","Yue Wang"],"upvotes":21,"id":"arxiv:2611.22275","source":"hf","date":"2026-11-18","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.22275","abstract":"Collaboration promises to extend humanoid capabilities beyond what individual robots can achieve. However, realizing this potential requires coordinated whole-body interactions, coherent long-horizon planning, and precise synchronization across robots. We introduce Heave-Ho, a decentralized framework for long-horizon collaborative multi-humanoid loco-manipulation. At the low level, we develop a multi-agent post-training framework that adapts a humanoid foundation tracking model to physically coupled interactions between humanoids, enabling coordinated decentralized control from local observations alone. At the high level, we train a vision-language planner and design a speech-based leader-follower coordination mechanism to synchronize decentralized execution without network communication. Trained with two identical humanoids, our system scales to teams of up to ten robots and generalizes"},{"topics":["Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23144","title":"$π^4$: A Foundation Model for 4D Scene Geometry and Motion","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23144","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23144.pdf","tags":["Evaluation"],"authors":["Yifan Wang","Weiguang Zhao","Ni Yang","Haoyu Guo","Junting Dong","Weinan Zhang","Chunhua Shen"],"upvotes":13,"id":"arxiv:2611.23144","source":"hf","date":"2026-11-18","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.23144","abstract":"$π^4$ is a unified foundation model for 4D scene perception. From diverse, uncalibrated RGB observations in the wild---a single image, an unordered collection, or a video sequence---it recovers dense geometry, camera poses, and metric scale, together with 2D/3D point tracks and moving-object masks for multi-image inputs. It couples a geometry branch with a geometry-conditioned motion branch, which predicts image-space correspondences that are lifted into 3D using reconstructed target-view geometry. Across diverse benchmarks, $π^4$ achieves the best overall average rank in geometry evaluation, alongside competitive performance in single- and multiview 2D/3D point tracking and moving-object segmentation. With a shared backbone, the full geometry-and-motion system contains approximately 0.5B parameters---less than half the size of most baselines. It produces joint reconstructions and tracks"},{"topics":["Evaluation","Reflection"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23219","title":"Do LLM Agents Still Need to Be Told How to Self-Improve?","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23219","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23219.pdf","tags":["Evaluation","Reflection"],"authors":["Shobhita Sundaram","Cansu Sancaktar","Phillip Isola"],"upvotes":5,"id":"arxiv:2611.23219","source":"hf","date":"2026-11-18","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.23219","abstract":"LLM agents increasingly drive their own learning. Self-improvement methods delegate various components of model optimization to the models themselves. As models become more capable, this motivates a basic question: do these increasingly capable models still need us to specify how they should self-improve? In this paper we study an autonomous paradigm in which, given basic primitives, resources, and a general goal for self-improvement, agents construct their own self-learning algorithm for context-based optimization. Using the setting of gameplay as a testbed, a frozen LLM agent is given a compute budget and tools for collecting experience and updating text documents, and allowed to freely experiment without an externally imposed loop or access to real-world data. We find that sufficiently capable models do self-improve under these conditions, demonstrating strategies that align with clas"},{"topics":["Tool Use","Reasoning","Evaluation","RL for LLM"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21791","title":"Regularized Score Centering","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21791","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-16","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.21791.pdf","tags":["Tool Use","Reasoning","Evaluation","RL for LLM"],"authors":["Hao Bai","Chenlu Ye","Xuanchang Zhang","Aviral Kumar","Tong Zhang"],"upvotes":4,"id":"arxiv:2611.21791","source":"hf","date":"2026-11-18","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.21791","abstract":"Asynchronous reinforcement learning increases training throughput, but data from stale policies and numerical mismatch between training and inference can destabilize learning. To stabilize off-policy learning, score centering (SC) removes the effect of reward baselines on the expected update. Our gradient diagnostics show less extreme gradients with SC than with importance sampling (IS), but SC can still fail when the learner moves far from the behavior policy that generated the data. To limit this policy mismatch, we introduce Regularized Score Centering (RSC), which adds a reverse Kullback-Leibler (KL) penalty to encourage the learner's next-token distribution to stay close to the behavior policy's next-token distribution. RSC works with or without truncated importance sampling (TIS), which caps token importance weights. Our theoretical analysis supports this approach, showing that KL "},{"topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","VLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","RL for LLM"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23034","title":"M3OPD-Reasoner: An Open Recipe for Post-Training Multimodal Reasoning with Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23034","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23034.pdf","tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","VLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"authors":["Nguyen Quang Trung","Hoang Phan","Shuo Sun","Longyin Zhang","AiTi Aw"],"upvotes":2,"id":"arxiv:2611.23034","source":"hf","date":"2026-11-18","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.23034","abstract":"Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation (MOPD) enables a single student to absorb the skills of several domain teachers by learning from its own generated responses. We present Multimodal Model Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation Reasoner (M3OPD-Reasoner), a fully open recipe for post-training vision-language models (VLMs) over three reasoning domains: image STEM, image perception, and video. We curate two datasets, M3OPD-SFT-1.35M for supervised fine-tuning (SFT) and M3OPD-RL-193K for reinforcement learning (RL) with verifiable rewards. On these data, we compare post-training recipes that start from Qwen3.5-9B: training one model on the mixed data of all domains (MixSFT and MixRL), and training one teacher per domain with Group Sequence Policy Optimization (GSPO), directly or after an SFT warm-up, and distilling the teachers into one student with MOPD. We evaluate on 21 image STEM, image "},{"topics":["Reasoning","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23026","title":"TPHC: Improving Hyper-Connections with Tensor-Parallel Grouped Updates","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23026","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23026.pdf","tags":["Reasoning","Evaluation"],"authors":["Xinying Hu","You Wu","Haoyi Wu","Jianchao Tan","Kefeng Zhang","Yuchen Xie","Kewei Tu"],"upvotes":2,"id":"arxiv:2611.23026","source":"hf","date":"2026-11-18","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.23026","abstract":"Hyper-Connections (HC) expand the residual stream of a Transformer into $N$ parallel streams, and manifold-constrained HC (mHC) stabilizes them at scale. However, mHC benefits little from more than four streams, partly because each sublayer writes a single output to all streams, limiting how differently they can be updated. In this work, we propose TPHC, which integrates tensor-parallel (TP) shards with HC streams to promote greater independence across streams. Specifically, we route a separate mixture of the residual streams to each TP shard and keep the individual shard outputs separate during write-back, so each stream can receive a different mixture of those outputs. This design makes the residual-stream updates more expressive without adding parameters or FLOPs to the attention or FFN sublayers. With native support for stream sharding under TP, TPHC requires only 9% more training ti"},{"topics":["Reasoning"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11937","title":"Attention at Absolute Zero","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11937","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.11937.pdf","tags":["Reasoning"],"authors":["Mostafa &#39;Neo&#39; Mohsenvand"],"upvotes":2,"id":"arxiv:2611.11937","source":"hf","date":"2026-11-18","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.11937","abstract":"Attention scores candidate supports of an answer, which we call witnesses, and reads the scores at a temperature. We build a higher-order attention layer whose state lives on pairs of items: for a pair (a, b), each third item k is a witness judged through its legs (a, k) and (k, b). It reads witnesses at zero temperature, keeping the best and the number tied with it, passes on only bits comparing such counts, rebuilds its pair state from the input and these bits at every loop, and is trained from answers alone. Each choice removes one way soft attention fails at size: dispersed weight, unresolved gaps between learned scores, and a drifting soft state. In maximum retrieval it is right on at least 99.95% of sets 1,000 times larger than in training, on all ten seeds; trained with a soft count, the same network falls to 42% at 4,096 items. On CLRS-30 without hints, a general form of the laye"},{"topics":["Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14505","title":"Continual-ARC: Measuring Skill Acquisition over a Working Lifetime","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14505","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-15","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14505.pdf","tags":["Evaluation"],"authors":["Daniel Lenton"],"upvotes":10,"id":"arxiv:2611.14505","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.14505","abstract":"Agent harnesses have emerged as a promising mechanism for continual learning, alongside test-time parametric learning. However, the current benchmarks have several limitations: they generally focus on a single applied domain, serve each instance only once, and assume a single family of learner, so memory-based and weight-based systems are rarely compared on the same scale. We propose Continual-ARC, a lifelong stream built from the three ARC-AGI editions, whose tasks need no domain knowledge. The same tasks return after dormant gaps with fresh instances, while new tasks keep arriving. On each instance, the learner may pay for demonstrations, submit answers, or act inside the harness, with retries permitted on incorrect submissions, which are paid for. Each instance is scored based on the share of this feedback budget consumed. Since a learner may keep anything between instances, memory-ba"},{"topics":["Reasoning","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22563","title":"CADMAGEDDON: High-Fidelity CAD Reconstruction with Local Inference","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22563","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22563.pdf","tags":["Reasoning","Evaluation"],"authors":["Oleg Pavlov","Nikita Gavrilov","Soslan Kabisov","Gennadii Savrasov","Andrey Kuznetsov","Anton Konushin","Dmitrii Zhemchuzhnikov"],"upvotes":8,"id":"arxiv:2611.22563","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.22563","abstract":"CAD practitioners often work with proprietary parts that they are unwilling to share with external services. For individuals and small companies, bringing this work in-house also requires computational resources they can afford. We introduce CADMAGEDDON, an open-source agent that reconstructs editable CAD from meshes and can run locally on single-GPU systems and current-generation laptops. The method decomposes this complex task into a chain of simple visual decisions about what to construct and what to correct. Geometric tools support precise construction, allowing the agent to focus on visual reasoning. Using the mid-sized Qwen3.8-27B model, CADMAGEDDON outperforms prior approaches, including systems based on frontier models, in reconstruction quality on all the main benchmarks. CADMAGEDDON integrates with FreeCAD, bringing its reconstructions into practitioners' existing CAD workflows"},{"topics":["Planning"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22565","title":"MillBill: A Machining Agent with Geometric Feedback","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22565","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22565.pdf","tags":["Planning"],"authors":["Gennadii Savrasov","Artem Slavin","Soslan Kabisov","Andrey Kuznetsov","Anton Konushin","Dmitrii Zhemchuzhnikov"],"upvotes":3,"id":"arxiv:2611.22565","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.22565","abstract":"Turning a CAD model into a machining program requires both process knowledge and geometric precision. Language models can generate such workflows by writing geometry and toolpath code for each part, but this makes numerical execution part of the model's task. We introduce MillBill, an agent that separates machining decisions from their geometric execution and verification. Given a boundary-representation part, stock, and material, a locally hosted, mid-sized open model proposes operations and cutting parameters for indexed three-axis machining. A geometric harness generates toolpaths, simulates material removal, and returns feedback for plan refinement. Setup planning accounts for what the available cutters can physically reach, rather than visibility alone. We evaluate the resulting programs by the fraction of the target surface area within the specified tolerance after simulated machin"},{"topics":["Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21644","title":"MInfoVisQA: A Multilingual Benchmark for Information-Rich Visual Question Answering","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21644","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-10-14","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.21644.pdf","tags":["Evaluation"],"authors":["Changjiang Jiang","Jiang Zhou","Xinbin Yuan","Fan Zhang","Tao Feng","Zhuohan Xie"],"upvotes":1,"id":"arxiv:2611.21644","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.21644","abstract":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) excel in vision-language tasks; yet, whether they maintain consistent understanding of information-rich visuals (InfoVis) when the surface language changes remains unclear. To quantify this capability, we introduce MInfoVisQA, a parallel benchmark with 8,960 QA configurations derived from 128 seed instances across 24 languages. We construct seed-matched, language-parallel visuals and QA pairs by disentangling numerical-structural data from text and programmatically synchronizing translated labels. We evaluate 25 MLLMs under two settings: Language-Aligned QA (LQA, matching visual-text languages) and Cross-Language QA (XQA, fixed QA language with varying visual languages). The results show that aggregate accuracy masks substantial robustness gaps: two models with comparable mean LQA accuracy (80.4\\%) exhibit performance spreads of 7.8 and 20.3 perce"},{"topics":["LLM Training"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23140","title":"GameRealm: Grounding Visual Learning in Interactive Games","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23140","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23140.pdf","tags":["LLM Training"],"authors":["Weiguang Zhao","Yifan Wang","Ni Yang","Yating Wang","Xingyu Miao","Yankai Jiang","Mulin Yu","Tao Lu","Haoyu Guo","Junting Dong","Weinan Zhang","Chunhua Shen"],"upvotes":1,"id":"arxiv:2611.23140","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.23140","abstract":"Visual foundation models are central to physical intelligence, yet their development remains constrained by fragmented training datasets, inconsistent annotation quality, and limited multimodal supervision. To address these challenges, we introduce GameRealm, a large-scale, richly annotated dataset that grounds visual observations in synchronized, game-native supervision. GameRealm encompasses over 120 million RGB frames and 1,000 hours of gameplay footage across all views. Beyond its unprecedented scale, GameRealm provides rich multimodal supervision, including metric depth, camera parameters, synchronized audio, control inputs, and 3D point trajectories. Its diversity further extends to flexible view configurations, spanning single-view, stereo, multi-view, and panoramic recordings. To enable scalable acquisition across diverse game environments, we develop a unified data collection pi"},{"topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Planning","Memory","Fine-tuning","Reflection","Agent Framework"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22418","title":"CutAgent: Creative-Gene-Driven Planning and Experience-Guided Self-Refinement for High-CTR E-commerce Advertising Video Creation","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22418","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22418.pdf","tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Planning","Memory","Fine-tuning"],"authors":["Xuyue Huang","Guanbo Huang","Jiasong Feng","Run Ling","Ao Ma","Xi Zhao","Yaoyu Li","Jie Gao","Ke Cao","Wei Feng","Zhanjie Zhang","Hao Wang","Jinghe Hu"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.22418","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"E-commerce advertising video creation requires both high-level creative planning and reliable multi-modal production. However, existing methods often map product attributes directly to scripts or editing decisions without explicitly modeling advertising strategies, narrative structures, and production styles. They also have limited ability to reuse past production failures. To address these limitations, we propose $\\textbf{CutAgent}$, a dual-agent framework that formulates advertising video creation as a Plan--Execute--Self-Evolve process. CutAgent comprises two collaborative agents, $\\textbf{GeneAgent}$ and $\\textbf{VideoAgent}$, connected by a structured creative gene that bridges product understanding and video production. GeneAgent maps multi-modal product inputs---textual attributes together with product images---to creative genes that specify narrative units and production rules. T"},{"topics":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Post-Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","RL for LLM"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23824","title":"Evidence-Based Credit Assignment for Search-Augmented Language Models","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23824","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23824.pdf","tags":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Post-Training","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"authors":["Wen Wang","Jindong Wang","Jiahua Bao","Tu Yongsiqi","Yihao Liu","Junwei He","Wenkui Fan"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.23824","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"Reinforcement learning from terminal answer rewards enables search-augmented language models to interleave reasoning and retrieval without step-level annotations, yet outcome supervision assigns identical credit to every search in a trajectory regardless of whether it retrieved useful information. This limitation is especially severe when all sampled rollouts for a question fail, collapsing group-centered outcome advantages to zero even when trajectories gather markedly different evidence. We present Evidence-based Credit Assignment (ECA), which augments terminal outcome rewards with a training-only auxiliary signal measuring how retrieved documents improve the likelihood of a reference answer. Rather than scoring the full dialogue prefix, ECA queries a frozen snapshot of the current policy under an evidence-only prompt containing solely the question and the deduplicated documents observ"},{"topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Evaluation","Alignment","Fine-tuning","Reflection"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23750","title":"Beyond Replacement: Levenshtein Editing for Diffusion Language Models","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23750","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23750.pdf","tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Evaluation","Alignment","Fine-tuning"],"authors":["Shuaidi Wang","Zhuocheng Gong","Xuejie Liu","Jianguo Li","Yu Zhang"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.23750","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"Diffusion language models (DLMs) offer a flexible alternative to autoregressive models, enabling any-order infilling. While most existing decoding methods treat decoded tokens as fixed, some recent models allow previously decoded tokens to be revised only by replacing them with alternative tokens, a paradigm we refer to as replacement-only editing (ROE). Although ROE can correct individual tokens, it is inefficient for sequence shifts caused by missing or extra tokens and struggles when errors occur across many positions. To address these limitations, we propose LED (Levenshtein Editing for Diffusion Language Models), a post-training framework that extends ROE with insertion and deletion, enabling Levenshtein-style refinement of generated sequences. It uses two training designs. First, it reconstructs edit supervision on the fly during supervised fine-tuning using longest-common-subseque"},{"topics":["Reasoning","Planning","Computer Use","Evaluation","RL for LLM"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22591","title":"PACT: Plan-Action Consistency Training by Preserving Correct Plans and Repairing Actions in GUI Agents","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22591","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22591.pdf","tags":["Reasoning","Planning","Computer Use","Evaluation","RL for LLM"],"authors":["Zhuohan Cai","Zhangxuan Gu","Runze Li","Beitong Zhou","Ming Wen","Hongbo Yu","Shuheng Shen","Changhua Meng"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.22591","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"GUI agents can formulate a correct immediate plan in their reasoning yet emit an action that fails to execute it, creating an execution gap. This recurring mismatch undermines reliable task completion, as a single execution error can derail a multi-step workflow. In outcome-supervised reinforcement learning, broadcasting a negative response-level advantage across all generated tokens risks penalizing valid planning together with erroneous execution. To address this credit-assignment conflict, we introduce PACT (Plan-Action Consistency Training), a training framework that combines reference-aligned plan protection and plan-aligned action repair without altering the original scalar action reward. Using annotated reference actions to identify execution gaps, PACT protects the verified plan by masking negative policy-gradient contributions across its token span. Keeping the generated reasoni"},{"topics":["VLM Training","Reasoning","Memory","Evaluation","Reflection"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23010","title":"PhysScope: Agentic Multimodal Physics Reasoning with Grounded Visual Memory","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23010","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23010.pdf","tags":["VLM Training","Reasoning","Memory","Evaluation","Reflection"],"authors":["Haoyang Liu","Kecheng Liang","Xinyu Li","Weixing Chen","Yang Liu"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.23010","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"Vision-language models (VLMs) can correctly interpret physics diagrams yet apply constraints outside the states or conditions in which they hold, causing premise errors to propagate through subsequent calculations. We introduce PhysScope, an inference-time visual harness that supports self-correction of these scope mismatches without updating model weights. PhysScope enables the model to link physical scopes directly to its visual observations and explicitly constructs derived views to support reasoning. The harness imposes explicit constraints on state distinctions, conditions, and physical scopes during model reasoning, and checks agent-proposed revisions against sources and scopes. Accepted revisions invalidate registered dependents, thereby maintaining scope-aware working memory. On PhysAlign, PhysScope achieves higher solving scores than direct solving across all six evaluated VLMs."},{"topics":["LLM Training","Reasoning","Planning","Fine-tuning","World Model"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22880","title":"Learning Agent-Centric Vision-Language-Action World Models for Autonomous Driving","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22880","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22880.pdf","tags":["LLM Training","Reasoning","Planning","Fine-tuning","World Model"],"authors":["Guoqing Wang","Pin Tang","Xiangxuan Ren","Chao Ma"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.22880","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"Recent progress in integrating vision-language-action (VLA) models with world modeling has significantly advanced end-to-end autonomous driving by allowing systems to reason about imagined futures instead of merely reacting to current observations. However, most world VLA models generate future frames at the pixel level with plenty of dense visual tokens. Although such generated futures can be visually plausible, they preserve many driving-irrelevant appearance details, i.e., only a small portion of the tokens directly encode critical planning-relevant factors, such as agent geometry, motion dynamics, and interactions. This introduces a significant performance bottleneck. An important question thus arises: what world state should a world VLA model reason over to support planning beyond pixel-level prediction? We argue that planning-oriented world modeling should focus on a compact and st"},{"topics":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","RL for LLM"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22438","title":"Eyes in the Update? Learning Where Rewards Land in Multimodal Reasoning","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22438","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22438.pdf","tags":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","RL for LLM"],"authors":["Lixuan He","Tianzhe Chu","Shulin Tian","Shikang Zheng","Feng Chen","Yi Ma"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.22438","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"Multimodal post-training has moved from broad instruction tuning toward reinforcement learning for image and video reasoning, where outcome rewards can improve final-answer accuracy. Yet these rewards are assigned to complete generated trajectories, while visual evidence is used only at sparse and uneven positions inside the response. Recent work has begun to make multimodal RL more token-sensitive through grounding signals or external token-level weighting. We ask a more specific question: can the allocation of the same trajectory-level reward be inferred from the model's own multimodal computation, rather than from an auxiliary annotator, perception module, or process label? We propose Gaze-RL, a representation-driven policy optimization method that redistributes completion-level advantage through a latent reward landing field. The field is computed from internal cross-modal geometry a"},{"topics":["Tool Use","Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","RL for LLM"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22161","title":"What RL on Expert-Built Tasks Teaches Nemotron 3.5 Super","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22161","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22161.pdf","tags":["Tool Use","Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","RL for LLM"],"authors":["Sushant Mehta","Edwin Chen"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.22161","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"We post-train NVIDIA's Nemotron 3.5 Super (120B total, 12B active parameters) with reinforcement learning (RL) on five expert-built task datasets, one at a time: real-world software engineering, complex constraints, long-horizon tool use, professional chart understanding and enterprise document reasoning. Each run trains a fresh rank-32 LoRA adapter with CISPO on Tinker, with no supervised stage, and each adapter is compared with the base model on the same tasks, harness and settings. On twelve headline benchmarks, eight built by other groups and four by Surge AI, the gains range from +4.5 points on IFBench to +13.8 on GDP_pdf, which rises from 10.4% to 24.2%; SWE-bench Pro V2 rises from 86.9% to 92.1%, Terminal-Bench 2.1 from 63.9% to 70.3%, MultiChallenge from 58.3% to 66.6%, AutomationBench from 14.8% to 27.5% and Chartography from 39.4% to 50.6%. The document adapter, trained without"},{"topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21646","title":"Limite 1B - Violetto: Technical Report","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21646","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-10-14","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.21646.pdf","tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning"],"authors":["Mario Prignano","Gabriele Cirillo","Alessio Morosini","Luca Cerovaz","Alessandro Bartolocci","Emanuele Rodolà","Giulio Starace","Francesco Pappone"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.21646","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"We present Limite 1B - Violetto, a one-billion-parameter model designed for high-throughput mathematical reasoning. Limite is trained from scratch on 237 billion tokens from a carefully curated, mathematics-centric corpus and supports context lengths of up to 131k tokens. Its architecture and training recipe were developed together to maximize reasoning capability while keeping training and inference costs low. Post-training combines knowledge distillation, targeted supervised fine-tuning, and RLVR on a curated curriculum. Limite achieves 94.01% on AIME 2026 and 74.25% on BeyondAIME, placing it among models many times its size on our evaluation suite. The model is deliberately only lightly instruction-tuned: it is intended primarily as a single-turn mathematical solver and as a fast, parallel component of larger systems, rather than as a general-purpose assistant. We release the model, i"},{"topics":["VLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","Alignment","RL for LLM"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23209","title":"Learning Grounded Reasoning from Human Gaze","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23209","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23209.pdf","tags":["VLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","Alignment","RL for LLM"],"authors":["Gabriel Sarch","Declan Campbell","Nicholas Budny","Kia Ghods","Katerina Fragkiadaki","Jonathan D. Cohen","Brenden M. Lake","Taylor W. Webb"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.23209","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"Human visual reasoning is driven by systematic exploration and extraction of task-relevant visual evidence. Human gaze provides a window into this process, making gaze a useful source of guidance for visually-grounded reasoning in vision-language models (VLMs). However, human gaze also includes noise and redundancies that may transfer poorly to VLMs. In this work, we investigate how human gaze data can be effectively leveraged to improve visual reasoning in VLMs, while maintaining alignment with human behavior. We collect 7,109 human gaze trials from a diverse and challenging set of visual reasoning tasks, and train grounded reasoning models with different forms of gaze guidance. Across evaluations in chart understanding, visual search, and counting, we find that using human gaze as a prior for grounded reasoning consistently outperforms supervision using the full gaze sequence. Based on"},{"topics":["VLM Training","Reasoning","Planning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23803","title":"CoT-WAM: Collaborative Training of Vision-Language Models and World-Action Models via Plan Latents","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23803","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23803.pdf","tags":["VLM Training","Reasoning","Planning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning"],"authors":["Jinhao Zhang","Yushen Zuo","Ke Rui","Weitao Zhou","Haibao Yu","Honglong Tian","Xiaoheng Ma","Jie Mei","Jiawei Wang","Minglei Li"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.23803","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"General robot manipulation requires both semantic competence and physical foresight. World action models (WAMs) acquire an understanding of and predictive capability over the physical world by jointly modeling visual foresight and action distributions. However, existing WAMs still lack semantic competence: they have limited ability to perform open-world scene understanding, semantic reasoning, and long-horizon planning. Although vision-language models (VLMs) provide these capabilities, effectively integrating their semantic priors into WAM prediction and control remains challenging. To address this challenge, we propose CoT-WAM, a jointly trained WAM-VLM framework. CoT-WAM uses a learnable embedding to exchange information between the VLM and the WAM, which is supervised by textual and goal-image signals during training to encode rich intent information. A tailored two-stage training str"},{"topics":["Reasoning","Fine-tuning","RL for LLM","Agent Framework"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22310","title":"EviDARL: Evidence-Centric Dual-Agent Reasoning via Alternating Reinforcement Learning for Multi-Table Question Answering","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22310","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22310.pdf","tags":["Reasoning","Fine-tuning","RL for LLM","Agent Framework"],"authors":["Dunhan Li","Leilei Gan","Lirong Gao","Wenyan Fan","Yongwei Wang","Sheng Guo","Fei Wu"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.22310","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"Despite advances in LLM-based table reasoning, most studies focus on single-table settings. Multi-table QA requires multi-hop reasoning over complex table relationships and evidence dispersed across long contexts, often without verifiable intermediate traces. To address these challenges, we introduce EviDA, an evidence-centric dual-agent framework comprising an Evidence Construction Agent and a Reasoning and Answering Agent. The Evidence Construction Agent generates and executes table-manipulation programs over complete tables to produce question-specific evidence and verifiable intermediate traces, simplifying subsequent reasoning. The Reasoning and Answering Agent then performs multi-hop reasoning over this evidence to derive final answers. We further introduce EviDARL, which combines agent-specific fine-grained rewards with parameter-efficient alternating reinforcement learning to imp"},{"topics":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Evaluation","Reflection"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22179","title":"RAG in a Trenchcoat: When Minimal Memory Is Enough for Agentic Systems, and When It Isn't","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22179","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22179.pdf","tags":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Evaluation","Reflection"],"authors":["Jingyu Liu","Zongze Li","Zach Xu","Zhanhui Zhou","Tahseen Rabbani","Dawn Song","Ce Zhang"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.22179","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"Agentic memory research has accumulated extraction, supersession, reflection, and graph synthesis on top of retrieval-augmented generation. However, we found that almost none of those primitives earn their cost in our tested memory benchmarks: we show this with BRIDGE, a deliberately minimal RAG memory with chained observation notes, hybrid BM25 with cosine retrieval, and chronological rendering, which reaches parity within bootstrap CI with the prior SOTA on LongMemEval-S and BEAM, and beats the best memory baseline on EverMemBench by 5.7-6.3 points at matched reader. It runs at ~5-7K reader-prompt tokens per query, using a single LLM call for each ingestion or query. We argue that this parity is itself the result: standard benchmarks score what memory retrieves, not what memory enables when the agent acts. To test the latter, we introduce five adversarial probes (Rule Application +-, P"},{"topics":["Planning","Evaluation","Reflection","Agent Framework"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22490","title":"SciWorkBench: A Benchmark for Real Scientific Workflows","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22490","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22490.pdf","tags":["Planning","Evaluation","Reflection","Agent Framework"],"authors":["Yuchen Zhang","Taolin Han","Jinghang Wang","Jinxin Wang","Yun Wu","Wai Yuet Chiu","Xiaogang Li","Yuting Fu","Kewei Sun","Jiaxin Li","Qile Jin","Jiajia Li","Ruodan Chen","Qingteng Chen","Jialu Nie","Junchao Li","Yifei Zhang","Chen Zhao","Hu Wei","Lin Qu","Yanni Yin","Bing Zhao"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.22490","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"Language models are increasingly being used as agents capable of planning and executing projects, as well as refining their actions through feedback. This shift raises a question: can current agents complete projects like real scientists? To examine this question, we introduce SciWorkBench, a benchmark for evaluating agents' abilities on real science tasks across biology, chemistry, materials science, and physics. SciWorkBench is built from peer-reviewed literature or expert-contributed research scenarios. We also propose a unified scientific analysis framework validated by domain experts, which can categorize most tasks in SciWorkBench. Each task covers one or more stages of this framework. We evaluate up-to-date models within the Claude Code and Codex agent frameworks under a unified execution and grading protocol. The best-performing agent achieves a mean score of 57.38, indicating th"},{"topics":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22069","title":"MedGuardBench: A Patient-Oriented Benchmark for Personalized Guardrail Development","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22069","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22069.pdf","tags":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning"],"authors":["Sara Khosravi","Xiaofei Wen","Majid Sarrafzadeh","Muhao Chen"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.22069","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"Safety is personal: the same action can be safe for one person but unsafe for another. Yet current guardrails judge content without considering who is affected. Using medication safety, we introduce \\textit{patient-conditioned medication guarding}, which predicts a safety verdict and 17 risk categories from a patient profile and prescription. We release \\textbf{MedGuardBench}, a drug-disjoint benchmark of 5{,}001 scenarios across 87 medications. We find that standard guardrails rely on surface cues, such as wording and profile presence, rather than patient facts. To address this, we develop \\textit{personalized reasoning guardrails} with three components. \\textbf{Personalized Reasoning Distillation} links risks to relevant patient fields. \\textbf{Context Unrolling (CU)} makes models check each field themselves, reducing false alarms on most backbones; controls show that providing the che"},{"topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Evaluation","Fine-tuning"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22307","title":"Learning to Edit IFC Building Models: Verifiable Checkers as the Training Signal for Dependency-Aware Editing Agents","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22307","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22307.pdf","tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Evaluation","Fine-tuning"],"authors":["Jiaxin Zhang","Yao Luo","Yuze Dan","Yunqin Li","Bowen Wang"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.22307","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"Editing a building information model in the Industry Foundation Classes (IFC) schema requires propagating changes to dependent elements while preserving unrelated entities. Existing language-model agents for IFC editing use prompted models without learning from edit outcomes. Target-file comparisons do not specify which alternative edits are allowed or which unrelated entities must remain unchanged. We present IfcGym, a CodeAct environment in which a language model edits IFC through executable code and four checkers verify validity relative to the input model, intent, dependency and preservation against a task specification. Their conjunction is strict success. IfcEdit distils verified teacher episodes into a Qwen3.5-9B student through supervised fine-tuning (SFT), then applies Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) with strict success as the reward. On IfcEditBench, 1,990 editing tas"},{"topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Reasoning","Fine-tuning"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23229","title":"Ambient LLMs: Data-Efficient Supervised Fine-tuning via Temperature Matching","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23229","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23229.pdf","tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Reasoning","Fine-tuning"],"authors":["Giannis Daras","Marios Papachristou","Adam Klivans","Antonio Torralba","Constantinos Daskalakis"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.23229","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"Collecting large-scale, diverse, and high-quality post-training datasets for autoregressive language models is challenging. Consequently, Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) is typically applied to datasets significantly smaller than those used for pre-training, which leads to overfitting and the catastrophic forgetting of the tails of the data distribution. To prevent this collapse, we introduce Ambient SFT, a principled framework for leveraging imperfect predictions to augment the fine-tuning set. In our method, a \"frozen\" version of the pre-trained model serves as a noisy teacher that the fine-tuned model must match at a coarse level. In particular, we require that the high-temperature versions of the conditional distributions produced by the post-training model should match the high-temperature versions of the pre-training ones. The temperature hyperparameter controls the level of trust in "},{"topics":["Post-Training","VLM Training","Alignment","Fine-tuning"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22495","title":"Motion-Aware Reward Modeling for Instruction-Guided Video Editing","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22495","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22495.pdf","tags":["Post-Training","VLM Training","Alignment","Fine-tuning"],"authors":["Li Li","Xiangbo Gao","Bo Ni","Samyadeep Basu","Viet Dac Lai","Jing Shi","Seunghyun Yoon","Gang Wu","Yu Wang","Thien Huu Nguyen","Tyler Derr","Franck Dernoncourt","Zhengzhong Tu","Yue Zhao"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.22495","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"Reward models for instruction-guided video editing can favor temporal consistency at the expense of motion. We study this failure, termed \\emph{motion collapse}, using controlled motion-suppression variants of real edits. On VE-Bench, the evaluated composite reward prefers a motion-suppressed candidate in $97\\%$ of cases. Learned video rewards and a VLM judge also struggle with mild suppression, while latent optimization and reward-guided fine-tuning exploit this weakness to increase reward as motion declines. A linear probe of our base reward backbone performs near chance on mild suppression, motivating an explicit inter-frame dynamics input. We propose a motion-aware reward model that combines an RGB frame-difference stream with preference training against motion-suppressed and fabricated-motion negatives. A distillation anchor preserves alignment with human quality judgments. On the V"},{"topics":["LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21799","title":"Chain-of-Thought Controllability Can Reduce Chain-of-Thought Monitorability","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21799","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-16","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.21799.pdf","tags":["LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning"],"authors":["Zheng-Xin Yong","Benjamin Arnav","Miles Wang","Tomek Korbak","Olivia Watkins"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.21799","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"Chain-of-thought (CoT) monitoring is a promising tool for detecting hidden goals, off-task behavior, and other failures in AI agents. One risk is that models could deliberately shape their reasoning to make it opaque to monitors. We stress-test this risk through persona-control training: supervised fine-tuning on mathematical reasoning traces rewritten in the voices of diverse personas. This induces a broader ability to control CoT, even though training contains no monitor-evasion examples. In covert side-task evaluations, traces in which a gpt-oss-20b model organism completes the hidden side task contain less monitorable evidence than the base model's. Averaged across evaluations and CoT lengths, weighted by side-task-success counts, the CoT monitorability metric g-mean$^2$ falls from 0.94 for the base model to 0.68 for the model organism without a hiding instruction, and to 0.22 when e"},{"topics":["Multi-Agent","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","World Model"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22691","title":"Making the World React Realistically: A Closed-Loop Interactive World Model for Autonomous Driving","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22691","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22691.pdf","tags":["Multi-Agent","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","World Model"],"authors":["Jia Hu","Yang Chang","Panqu Wang","Zhijun Fu","Chang Liu","Changjian Yu","Jintao Lai"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.22691","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"World models (WMs) generate driving scenarios beyond recorded logs, enabling the training and evaluation of autonomous driving systems in unseen interactions. However, existing WMs struggle to ensure both behavioral realism and visual fidelity, since they overlook the relationship between vehicle interactions and the ego visual observations. We propose a closed-loop interactive WM that couples behavior modeling and visual rendering. First, imitation-learned background-vehicle (BV) policies are fine-tuned with multi-agent reinforcement learning in the closed loop against an end-to-end ego planner that reacts to the rendered images, so that their interaction behaviors match those in real logs. Second, the viewpoints exposed by these interactions guide a per-Gaussian diagnosis of each 3D Gaussian vehicle asset, which attenuates view-dependent colors on under-observed Gaussians and fills nev"},{"topics":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Tool Use","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23225","title":"StringQFT: A Multiuse Textbook Resource for AI-Assisted Theoretical Physics","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23225","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23225.pdf","tags":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Tool Use","Evaluation"],"authors":["Xingyang Yu"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.23225","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"We present StringQFT as a textbook collection for string constructions of quantum field theory and as infrastructure for AI-assisted theoretical physics. The collection comprises 18 volumes, 190 chapters and 3,795 pages, covering brane probes, brane webs, geometric engineering, F-theory constructions, wrapped M5-branes and exact worldsheet constructions. Its open-book benchmark inventory contains 1,073 items, including 499 with automatic finite-answer checking. For retrieval-augmented generation, it provides 1,371 section records linked to textbook sources and PDF locations, together with a local lexical retriever. Training-data preparation is illustrated by 21 private, source-linked examples. For agents, it exposes 19 calculation tools with specified inputs and finite domains, connecting tool use to textbook derivations. We illustrate these uses through specific questions, retrieved pas"},{"topics":["VLM Training","Evaluation","Reflection","RL for LLM"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22854","title":"WATERFALL: Workflow for Adaptive Training with Evolutionary Reward Formulation and Automated Learning Loops","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22854","announceDate":"2026-11-18","submittedDate":"2026-11-17","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22854.pdf","tags":["VLM Training","Evaluation","Reflection","RL for LLM"],"authors":["Eleftherios Triantafyllidis","Filippos Christianos","Zhibin Li","Bernd Bickel"],"upvotes":0,"id":"arxiv:2611.22854","source":"arxiv","date":"2026-11-18","abstract":"Reward design remains a central bottleneck in reinforcement learning (RL), particularly in sparse-reward, long-horizon and partially observable settings requiring dependency chaining, memory, and deceptive affordance disambiguation. While recent foundation-model approaches reduce manual reward engineering, most assume goal-conditioning, task descriptions, expert demonstrations, or ground-truth metrics. These assumptions are problematic in partially observable environments, where task-relevant objectives, affordances, and dynamics must be discovered through interaction rather than disclosed a priori. We formalise this problem setting by introducing WATERFALL, an iterative population-based workflow for automated reward discovery under the strict observability constraints inherent in POMDP environments. WATERFALL achieves this by (i) synthesising diverse programmatic reward candidates via p"}],"archive":[],"latestPath":"data/latest.json"};
